/*--------------------------------------------
 Reduced eye (Schwartz), bare

 The reduced eye of Schwartz, Geometrical and Visual Optics: A Clinical Introduction, chapter 7 (Figure 7-1): one spherical refracting
 surface, R = +5.55 mm, between air (n = 1.000) and aqueous (n' = 1.333). Power (1.333 - 1)/0.00555 = 60.00 D exactly; focal lengths
 -16.67 and +22.22 mm (1.333/60 = 22.2167 mm, which the book rounds to 22.22), so the axial length is made 22.2167 mm and the eye is
 exactly emmetropic. The nodal point is at the centre of curvature; the principal planes are on the surface. Nothing is in front of it.
 (The curved retina is drawn for the diagram; the book gives only the axial length.)
 The laboratory versions add slots for a correcting, an ametropia and an accommodating lens (they are in "Clinical models").
 For another published reduced eye see Emsley's (R 5.556 mm, n = 4/3, from TEVOI).
 The 5 mm stop is the one the laboratory versions use.
----------------------------------------------*/
